      "text": "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a Cartesian coordinate system, we can use the distance formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\), we can substitute these coordinates into the formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(4 - (-3))^2 + (-5 - (-4))^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expressions inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(4 + 3)^2 + (-5 + 4)^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{7^2 + (-1)^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the squares:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{49 + 1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{50}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the square root:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{25} \\times \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) is \\(\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}\\).",
